      {
        "id": "p34-double-inequalities-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$1 \\le 2x+5 \\le 9$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-2 \\le x \\le 2$.",
          "worked": "At the boundary $y=1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+5&=1\\\\2x&=-4\\\\x&=-2\\end{align*}$$\nAt the boundary $y=9$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+5&=9\\\\2x&=4\\\\x&=2\\end{align*}$$\nThe line $y=2x+5$ is increasing and lies between the two boundaries for $-2 \\le x \\le 2$. Both endpoints are included.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-double-inequalities-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$0 \\le 4-2x < 10$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-3 < x \\le 2$.",
          "worked": "At the boundary $y=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}4-2x&=0\\\\-2x&=-4\\\\x&=2\\end{align*}$$\nAt the boundary $y=10$:\n$$\\begin{align*}4-2x&=10\\\\-2x&=6\\\\x&=-3\\end{align*}$$\nThe line $y=4-2x$ is decreasing. Exclude $x=-3$, where $y=10$, and include $x=2$, where $y=0$. Therefore, $-3 < x \\le 2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-double-inequalities-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$-1 < \\frac{x}{2}+1 < 3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-4 < x < 4$.",
          "worked": "At the boundary $y=-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x}{2}+1&=-1\\\\\\frac{x}{2}&=-2\\\\x&=-4\\end{align*}$$\nAt the boundary $y=3$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x}{2}+1&=3\\\\\\frac{x}{2}&=2\\\\x&=4\\end{align*}$$\nThe line $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1$ is increasing. Both inequalities are strict, so both endpoints are excluded. Therefore, $-4 < x < 4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-double-inequalities-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$-1 < \\frac{4-3x}{2} \\le 5$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
