              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "At the boundary $y=-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{4-3x}{2}&=-1\\\\4-3x&=-2\\\\-3x&=-6\\\\x&=2\\end{align*}$$\nAt the boundary $y=5$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{4-3x}{2}&=5\\\\4-3x&=10\\\\-3x&=6\\\\x&=-2\\end{align*}$$\nThe line $y=\\frac{4-3x}{2}$ is decreasing. Include $x=-2$, where $y=5$, and exclude $x=2$, where $y=-1$. Therefore, $-2 \\le x < 2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
